Chapter: Capability

 The capability table, once. A capability used to be written out five
 times: the vocabulary the type checker admits, the identifier the CDX
 manifest carries, the kernel bits the boot grant expands, the kernel
 bits the verified loader expands, and the bit numbers themselves, which
 existed twice under two names. Four of those live in the compiler and
 the fifth lives in the operating system, and no single compile ever saw
 more than one quire at a time -- so the agreement between them was
 checked by a build script rather than held by the language.

 Measured before this chapter existed: the compiler's `cap-*` constants
 and the loader's `kern-cap-*` constants were identical in all 23 names
 AND all 23 values. That is not a coincidence anyone maintained on
 purpose; it is a coincidence `build/check-cap-tables.ps1` defended, and
 the source called that "not a fix, only a truce".

 This is where the truce ends. The table is here because the foreword is
 the only quire both sides may cite: Library Rule 2 fixes the order
 `codex.foreword -> codex -> codex.os`, so the compiler and the loader
 can each cite this and neither has to cite the other. A binary now gets
 the same authority whichever door it came in through, by construction
 rather than by a script that noticed when it did not.

 What this chapter deliberately does NOT do is derive the foreword
 `effect <Name> where` declarations. An effect declaration is a syntax
 form carrying its own operation signatures, and no table can produce
 one; `build/check-effect-vocab.ps1` guards that list and is the only
 instrument that ever could. That guard stays.

Section: Kernel Capability Bits

 The bit positions the kernel grants authority in. These were the
 compiler's `cap-*` family and the loader's `kern-cap-*` family; the
 names here are the compiler's, because it had 55 uses across six files
 against the loader's 17 in one, so keeping this spelling left the
 larger side untouched.

 The gaps are real. Bits 1, 2 and 13 are unassigned, and FileSystem and
 Network have no base bit at all -- they are read/write only, so a bare
 grant is the union of the two and never a third thing.

  cap-console : Integer = 0
  cap-concurrent : Integer = 3
  cap-console-read : Integer = 4
  cap-console-write : Integer = 5
  cap-filesystem-read : Integer = 6
  cap-filesystem-write : Integer = 7
  cap-network-read : Integer = 8
  cap-network-write : Integer = 9
  cap-block-device : Integer = 10
  cap-ipc : Integer = 11
  cap-process-create : Integer = 12
  cap-capability-admin : Integer = 14
  cap-identity : Integer = 15
  cap-device : Integer = 16
  cap-gpu-compute : Integer = 17
  cap-gpu-memory : Integer = 18
  cap-camera : Integer = 19
  cap-microphone : Integer = 20
  cap-location : Integer = 21
  cap-sensors : Integer = 22
  cap-display : Integer = 23
  cap-flash : Integer = 24
  cap-audio : Integer = 25
  cap-process : Integer = 26

Section: Directions

 A grant is read, write, or both. The compiler wrote these as
 `manifest-dir-*` and the CDX format wrote them as `cdx-dir-*`, with the
 same three values, and this section used to say that both AGREED with
 these -- which was true, and was true the way the `cap-*` and
 `kern-cap-*` families were true before this chapter existed: by
 inspection, held by nobody.

 That is now closed the same way the rest of the table was.
 `manifest-dir-*` is deleted and the compiler reads these names directly.
 `cdx-dir-*` keeps its spelling, because it genuinely names a byte in a
 file format rather than a value, but it is defined from these and is no
 longer a second copy.

 The reason it mattered is that a drifted triple could not announce
 itself. `cap-dir-bits` below is the only reader of a direction anywhere,
 and its last branch is readwrite: a renumbered `write` does not fail a
 comparison, it falls through and expands into read AND write. The boot
 path and the verified-load path would have handed the same binary
 different authority, which is the failure this whole chapter exists to
 make unreachable.

  cap-dir-read : Integer = 0
  cap-dir-write : Integer = 1
  cap-dir-readwrite : Integer = 2

Section: The Table

 One row per capability, keyed by the identifier the CDX manifest
 carries. A row names its base bit, its read and write bits if the
 family has a direction, and any further bits the family implies.
 Negative one means the row has no such bit, which is a statement and
 not a placeholder: FileSystem has no base bit, and Concurrent has no
 direction.

 There are 18 rows and the type checker's vocabulary has 24 entries.
 The difference is not drift. Console, FileSystem and Network each admit
 a dotted refinement in an effect row (`[FileSystem.Read]`), and those
 six names are DERIVED below rather than stored, because a refinement is
 a direction on a capability and not a capability of its own.

 Process is the newest row and the reason it was missing is worth
 keeping. `[Process]` guards `get-args`, `get-env`, `current-dir`,
 `run-process`, `run-process-full` and `process-exit` -- host authority,
 and `run-process` in particular is arbitrary command execution, which
 is why it takes a bit of its own rather than borrowing Concurrent's
 `cap-process-create`. That bit is the bare-metal spawn authority and a
 different thing. Until this row existed the whole family was dead on
 arrival: every one of those builtins resolved and type-checked, and no
 program could declare the row needed to call any of them, because
 CDX4001 refused a name the vocabulary did not carry. Nothing in
 `codex/test` or `codex/os` declares `[Process]`, which is why an entire
 effect family being uncallable went unnoticed.

 Like Concurrent and Capability, Process has no `effect Process where`
 declaration in the foreword -- its operations are compiler builtins,
 typed in `Types/Builtins.codex` rather than by a declaration. That
 asymmetry is deliberate and `build/check-effect-vocab.ps1` carries it in
 its `capsWithoutEffect` list, so it is a recorded decision and not a
 silence.

  CapSpec = record {
   cs-name : Text,
   cs-id : Integer,
   cs-base-bit : Integer,
   cs-read-bit : Integer,
   cs-write-bit : Integer,
   cs-extra-bits : List Integer
  }

  capability-table : List CapSpec
  capability-table = [
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Console", cs-id = 0, cs-base-bit = cap-console, cs-read-bit = cap-console-read, cs-write-bit = cap-console-write, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "FileSystem", cs-id = 1, cs-base-bit = 0 - 1, cs-read-bit = cap-filesystem-read, cs-write-bit = cap-filesystem-write, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Network", cs-id = 2, cs-base-bit = 0 - 1, cs-read-bit = cap-network-read, cs-write-bit = cap-network-write, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Concurrent", cs-id = 3, cs-base-bit = cap-concurrent, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [cap-ipc, cap-process-create] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Device", cs-id = 4, cs-base-bit = cap-block-device, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [cap-device] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Gpu.Compute", cs-id = 5, cs-base-bit = cap-gpu-compute, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Gpu.Memory", cs-id = 6, cs-base-bit = cap-gpu-memory, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Identity", cs-id = 7, cs-base-bit = cap-identity, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Capability", cs-id = 8, cs-base-bit = cap-capability-admin, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Gpu", cs-id = 9, cs-base-bit = 0 - 1, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [cap-gpu-compute, cap-gpu-memory] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Camera", cs-id = 10, cs-base-bit = cap-camera, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Microphone", cs-id = 11, cs-base-bit = cap-microphone, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Location", cs-id = 12, cs-base-bit = cap-location, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Sensors", cs-id = 13, cs-base-bit = cap-sensors, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Display", cs-id = 14, cs-base-bit = cap-display, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Flash", cs-id = 15, cs-base-bit = cap-flash, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Audio", cs-id = 16, cs-base-bit = cap-audio, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Process", cs-id = 17, cs-base-bit = cap-process, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] }
  ]

Section: The Expansion

 One expansion, read by the boot grant and by the verified loader. A
 family with no direction takes readwrite when it is asked for one,
 which is what both hand-written expansions did: withholding a bit the
 family does not model would deny an authority the program correctly
 declared.

  cap-or-bits : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-or-bits (bs) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else cap-or-bits bs (i + 1) len (bit-or acc (bit-shl 1 (list-at bs i)))

  cap-dir-bits : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-dir-bits (dir) (read-bit) (write-bit) =
   if dir == cap-dir-read then bit-shl 1 read-bit
   else if dir == cap-dir-write then bit-shl 1 write-bit
   else bit-or (bit-shl 1 read-bit) (bit-shl 1 write-bit)

  cap-bits-for-spec : CapSpec, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bits-for-spec (spec) (dir) =
   let base = if spec.cs-base-bit >= 0 then bit-shl 1 (spec.cs-base-bit) else 0
   in let dirb = if spec.cs-read-bit >= 0 then cap-dir-bits dir (spec.cs-read-bit) (spec.cs-write-bit) else 0
   in let extra = cap-or-bits (spec.cs-extra-bits) 0 (list-length (spec.cs-extra-bits)) 0
   in bit-or base (bit-or dirb extra)

Section: Lookups

 A miss answers negative one for an identifier and zero for a bit mask.
 Zero is the honest answer for an unknown capability: no authority. Both
 hand-written expansions ended in exactly that and this keeps it.

  cap-find-by-name : List CapSpec, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-find-by-name (ts) (n) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at ts i).cs-name == n then i
   else cap-find-by-name ts n (i + 1) len

  cap-find-by-id : List CapSpec, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-find-by-id (ts) (id) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at ts i).cs-id == id then i
   else cap-find-by-id ts id (i + 1) len

  cap-id-for-name : Text -> Integer
  cap-id-for-name (n) =
   let ts = capability-table
   in let i = cap-find-by-name ts n 0 (list-length ts)
   in if i < 0 then 0 - 1 else (list-at ts i).cs-id

  cap-bits-for-name : Text, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bits-for-name (n) (dir) =
   let ts = capability-table
   in let i = cap-find-by-name ts n 0 (list-length ts)
   in if i < 0 then 0 else cap-bits-for-spec (list-at ts i) dir

  cap-bits-for-id : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bits-for-id (id) (dir) =
   let ts = capability-table
   in let i = cap-find-by-id ts id 0 (list-length ts)
   in if i < 0 then 0 else cap-bits-for-spec (list-at ts i) dir

Section: The Vocabulary

 The names the type checker admits in an effect row: every capability,
 plus a dotted Read and Write for the three families that have a
 direction. 18 rows give 24 names.

 This is a membership test at every call site, so the order is not
 load-bearing and this does not reproduce the order the hand-written
 list happened to be in.

 capability-table is a zero-parameter definition, so a mention of one is
 a call that rebuilds the table. Every function above binds it to a
 local exactly once for that reason. 18 rows is small enough that the
 rebuild does not matter at the rate these are called, which is per
 declared effect and not per emitted instruction -- but the shape is the
 same one that made emit-builtin expensive, so bind it, do not mention
 it twice.

  cap-names-from : List CapSpec, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  cap-names-from (ts) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let s = list-at ts i
   in let a1 = list-push acc (s.cs-name)
   in if s.cs-read-bit >= 0
      then cap-names-from ts (i + 1) len (list-push (list-push a1 (s.cs-name & ".Read")) (s.cs-name & ".Write"))
      else cap-names-from ts (i + 1) len a1

  capability-names : List Text
  capability-names =
   let ts = capability-table
   in cap-names-from ts 0 (list-length ts) []
